"""Scene definitions for the Tokens in the Browser with Spring Boot: a BFF and DPoP teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Tokens in the Browser with Spring Boot: a BFF and DPoP',
         body=None,
         narration='Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains keeping tokens out of the browser, with a backend for the frontend and D Po P, on Spring Boot, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] A web page that keeps tokens in local storage hands them to every script on the page. [[slnc 300]] A backend for the frontend, a B F F, keeps the tokens on the server, and gives the browser only a cookie that scripts cannot read. [[slnc 300]] And where a token must reach the browser, D Po P binds it to a key, so a copy alone is useless. [[slnc 600]] Think of leaving your house key with the concierge, instead of under the doormat. [[slnc 600]] Our example is an online bank.'),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=["An online bank's single-page app", 'calls its accounts API.', '', 'The demo starts a real Keycloak in Docker,', 'the real accounts API and a real BFF,', 'and lets a third-party script on the page', 'do what scripts do.'],
         narration='Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online bank has a single-page web app that calls its accounts A P I. [[slnc 500]] The demo starts a real Keycloak in Docker, the real accounts A P I, and a real backend for the frontend, all on Spring Boot. [[slnc 500]] It lets a third-party script on the page do what such scripts can do, and compares what each design leaves it.'),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['localStorage: browser storage any script can read', 'HttpOnly cookie: sent by the browser, unreadable by scripts', 'BFF: a backend that holds the tokens', 'DPoP: a token bound to a client key', 'Proof: a signed note for one request'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] Local storage is browser storage that any script on the page can read. [[slnc 500]] An H T T P only cookie is sent by the browser, but scripts cannot read it. [[slnc 500]] A backend for the frontend is a server that holds the tokens for the page. [[slnc 500]] D Pop binds a token to a key held by the client. [[slnc 500]] And a proof is a small signed note, made for one request.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['SPA: Single-Page Application: one page that calls APIs from the browser', "API: Application Programming Interface: here, the bank's accounts service", 'OAuth, OAuth2: Open Authorization 2.0: the standard for handing out access tokens', 'BFF: Backend For the Frontend: a server that holds the tokens for the page', 'DPoP: Demonstrating Proof of Possession (RFC 9449): a token bound to a key', 'JWT, JWK, JSON: JSON Web Token; JSON Web Key; JavaScript Object Notation'],
         narration="A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] S P A is a single page application: one web page that calls A P Is from the browser. [[slnc 400]] A P I is an application programming interface. Here, the bank's accounts service. [[slnc 400]] O Auth is open authorization: the standard way to hand out access tokens. [[slnc 400]] B F F is a backend for the frontend: a server that holds the tokens for the page. [[slnc 400]] D Pop is demonstrating proof of possession: a token that works only with a fresh proof from one key. [[slnc 400]] J W T is a J son web token, a signed token in three parts. J W K is a J son web key. J son is JavaScript object notation."),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['SHA-256: Secure Hash Algorithm, 256 bits: 32 bytes from any text', 'ES256, P-256, EC: ECDSA with SHA-256 on the P-256 elliptic curve (EC)', 'ECDSA: Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm', 'base64url: bytes written with letters, digits, - and _, safe in a URL', 'iat, exp, sub, azp, jti: issued at, expires, subject, authorized party, token id', "cnf.jkt, htm, htu, ath: key thumbprint; a proof's method, URL, token hash"],
         narration="S H A two fifty six is the secure hash algorithm: it turns any text into thirty two bytes. [[slnc 400]] E S two fifty six is an elliptic curve signature with S H A two fifty six, on the curve called P two fifty six. [[slnc 400]] E C D S A is the elliptic curve digital signature algorithm. [[slnc 400]] Base sixty four U R L writes bytes with letters, digits, a dash and an underscore. [[slnc 400]] In a token, i a t is issued at, e x p is expires, s u b is the subject, a z p the client it was issued to, and j t i a unique id. [[slnc 400]] c n f dot j k t is the bound key's thumbprint. In a proof, h t m is the method, h t u the address, and a t h the hash of the access token."),
    dict(key='06-letters-3', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=["CSRF, XSRF: Cross-Site Request Forgery; XSRF-TOKEN is Spring's cookie against it", 'PKCE: Proof Key for Code Exchange: a secret that ties the sign-in to the client', 'HTTP, HTTPS: Hypertext Transfer Protocol, and its encrypted form', 'URL, UUID: Uniform Resource Locator; Universally Unique Identifier', 'CORS: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing: the browser asks first', 'CSP: Content Security Policy: which scripts a page may run'],
         narration='C S R F, also written X S R F, is cross site request forgery: another site making the browser send a request. [[slnc 400]] P K C E is proof key for code exchange. [[slnc 400]] H T T P is the hypertext transfer protocol. H T T P S is its encrypted form. [[slnc 400]] U R L is a uniform resource locator, a web address. U U I D is a universally unique identifier. [[slnc 400]] Cors is cross origin resource sharing. The browser asks the server first, in a preflight, before some requests from another site. [[slnc 400]] C S P is a content security policy: the list of scripts a page may run.'),
    dict(key='07-letters-4', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['RFC: Request for Comments: an internet standard'],
         narration='R F C is request for comments: the name of an internet standard.'),
    dict(key='08-what-scripts-read', kind='figure', title='What a script on the page can read',
         body='what_scripts_read',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] The same third party script runs on the bank's page, twice. [[slnc 700]] On the left, the app keeps the tokens Keycloak gave it in local storage. [[slnc 300]] Any script on the page can read local storage. [[slnc 300]] So the script takes both tokens, and sends them to its own server. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the tokens stay on the backend for the frontend. [[slnc 300]] Local storage is empty. [[slnc 300]] The session cookie is H T T P only, so document dot cookie shows only the X S R F token, which is no token at all. [[slnc 300]] The script takes nothing. [[slnc 700]] Anything a script on the page can read, it can send away. [[slnc 300]] So the safest token is one the page never holds."),
    dict(key='09-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='Two stolen tokens, as long as the session lives',
         body='token_timeline',
         narration="First demo: tokens kept in the browser. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the access token's claims, decoded from its middle part. [[slnc 300]] The subject is Ana's id at Keycloak. [[slnc 300]] The authorized party is the app, bank S P A. [[slnc 300]] The scope: read accounts, and make payments. [[slnc 300]] And it expires three hundred seconds after it was issued. [[slnc 700]] Below, a timeline of what the thief does with the two stolen tokens. [[slnc 300]] At once, the access token reads Ana's balance. [[slnc 300]] At three hundred and thirty one seconds, Spring's resource server refuses it: invalid token. [[slnc 600]] But the refresh token lives for the whole session, eighteen hundred seconds of idle time, and every use starts the clock again. [[slnc 300]] The thief trades it at Keycloak for a new access token, and reads the balance again. [[slnc 700]] One visit to a page with one bad script, for as long as the session lives."),
    dict(key='10-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — Tokens kept in the browser',
         body="ONE. Tokens kept in the browser.\n  THE SIGN-IN (Keycloak, client bank-spa; both go to localStorage):\n    access_token   <a JWT signed by Keycloak>\n    refresh_token  <a JWT signed by Keycloak>\n  THE ACCESS TOKEN'S CLAIMS (its middle part, decoded):\n    sub    a0a0a0a0-0000-4000-8000-000000000001\n    azp    bank-spa  (the client it was issued to)\n    scope  accounts.read openid payments.write\n    exp    = iat + 300 seconds (five minutes)\n  THE REFRESH TOKEN'S CLAIMS:\n    exp    = iat + 1800 seconds (the session's idle time)\n  THE APP'S OWN REQUEST (Authorization: Bearer <access_token>):\n    GET /accounts -> 200 balance 125000 cents",
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The sign in, with both tokens in local storage. [[slnc 400]] The access token's claims, and its life worked out: issued at, plus three hundred seconds. [[slnc 300]] The refresh token: plus eighteen hundred. [[slnc 400]] And the app's own request: two hundred, with the balance."),
    dict(key='11-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — Tokens kept in the browser',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE SCRIPT ON THE PAGE READS localStorage:\n    access_token, refresh_token -> 2 tokens sent to its server\n  THE STOLEN TOKENS, USED FROM ELSEWHERE (Ana closed the page):\n    access token, at once        -> 200 balance 125000 cents\n    access token, 331 s later    -> 401 (invalid_token)\n    refresh token, at Keycloak   -> a new access token\n    the new access token         -> 200 balance 125000 cents\n  ONE PAGE VISIT, AS LONG AS THE SESSION LIVES:\n    anything a script can read, a script can send away',
         narration='The script reads local storage, and sends two tokens to its server. [[slnc 400]] The stolen access token works at once, and is refused three hundred and thirty one seconds later. [[slnc 300]] The refresh token gets a new one, and the balance again. [[slnc 400]] Anything a script can read, a script can send away.'),
    dict(key='12-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='A cookie in the browser, the tokens on the server',
         body='bff_layout',
         narration="Second demo: a backend for the frontend, built with Spring Boot. [[slnc 400]] At the top, three parts. [[slnc 300]] Ana's browser holds no tokens. [[slnc 300]] The backend for the frontend keeps two tokens in its session, with Spring's authorized client service. [[slnc 300]] It adds the access token on every call to the accounts A P I. [[slnc 700]] Bottom left, the session cookie. [[slnc 300]] Its value is only a session id. [[slnc 300]] Secure: over H T T P S only. [[slnc 300]] H T T P only: scripts cannot read it. [[slnc 300]] Same site strict: it stays home when another site starts the request. [[slnc 300]] And the host prefix: no other host can set it. [[slnc 700]] Bottom right, the same script on the page. [[slnc 300]] While the page is open, it can still ask for the balance. [[slnc 300]] What it took is useless anywhere else."),
    dict(key='13-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — A backend for the frontend keeps the tokens on the server',
         body="TWO. A backend for the frontend keeps the tokens on the server.\n  THE SIGN-IN (Spring's oauth2Login on the BFF, client bank-bff):\n    Set-Cookie: __Host-bff=<session id>\n      Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict\n    Set-Cookie: XSRF-TOKEN=<random>; Path=/\n  THE COOKIE'S RULES (__Host-bff):\n    Secure           sent over HTTPS only\n    HttpOnly         scripts on the page cannot read it\n    SameSite=Strict  not sent when another site starts it\n    __Host- prefix   Secure, Path=/, no Domain: true",
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The sign in: the session cookie, and the X S R F token cookie. [[slnc 400]] And the session cookie's four rules, each with what it means."),
    dict(key='14-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — A backend for the frontend keeps the tokens on the server',
         body="TWO. (continued)\n  WHERE THE TOKENS ARE:\n    the BFF's OAuth2AuthorizedClientService  2 tokens\n    the browser                              0 tokens\n  THE APP'S OWN REQUEST (only the cookie goes with it):\n    GET /api/accounts -> 200 balance 125000 cents",
         narration="Where the tokens are: two on the server, none in the browser. [[slnc 400]] The app's own request carries only the cookie: two hundred."),
    dict(key='15-act-2-3', kind='console', title='Act Two — A backend for the frontend keeps the tokens on the server',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  THE SAME SCRIPT ON THE PAGE READS document.cookie:\n    [XSRF-TOKEN] -> 0 tokens; __Host-bff is not readable: true\n  WHAT THE SCRIPT CAN STILL DO:\n    page open    GET /api/accounts -> 200 balance 125000 cents\n      <- A SCRIPT ON THE PAGE CAN STILL ACT AS ANA\n    page closed  what it took, from elsewhere -> 401\n  SIX MINUTES LATER (the access token has expired):\n    the browser sends the cookie -> 200 balance 125000 cents\n    the BFF refreshed it on the server, a new token: true',
         narration="The script reads document dot cookie, and sees only the X S R F token. [[slnc 300]] No tokens. [[slnc 300]] But while the page is open it can still read the balance. [[slnc 400]] Six minutes later the server refreshes the token, out of the browser's sight."),
    dict(key='16-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='Who starts the request, and what goes with it',
         body='cross_site',
         narration="Third demo: requests started by another site. [[slnc 400]] Each row is one attempt, while Ana is signed in. [[slnc 300]] The middle columns say what the browser attaches: the session cookie, and the X S R F token header. [[slnc 700]] A link and a form on another site, in a modern browser. [[slnc 300]] Same site strict keeps the cookie off both. [[slnc 600]] The same form in an older browser. [[slnc 300]] The cookie goes, but a form cannot add a header, so Spring's C S R F check refuses it. [[slnc 600]] A script on the other site tries to add the header. [[slnc 300]] The browser asks the server first, and the answer allows no other origin. [[slnc 300]] So the request is never sent. [[slnc 600]] Last row: the bank's own page sends both. [[slnc 300]] One transfer is made."),
    dict(key='17-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — Requests started by another site',
         body='THREE. Requests started by another site.\n  THE REQUESTS (started on evil.example, Ana signed in):\n    a link   GET /api/accounts\n    a form   POST /api/transfers, no X-XSRF-TOKEN header\n  THE RESPONSES (a modern browser: SameSite=Strict, no cookie):\n    the link -> 401 (not signed in)\n    the form -> 403 (no session, no CSRF token)\n  THE SAME FORM IN AN OLDER BROWSER (it ignores SameSite):\n    the cookie goes, the header does not -> 403 (no CSRF token)',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The requests from the other site. [[slnc 300]] In a modern browser: four oh one for the link, four oh three for the form. [[slnc 300]] In an older browser: four oh three, no C S R F token.'),
    dict(key='18-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — Requests started by another site',
         body="THREE. (continued)\n  A SCRIPT ON evil.example ADDS X-XSRF-TOKEN ITSELF:\n    the browser asks first (a CORS preflight)\n    Access-Control-Allow-Origin in the answer: false\n      -> never sent\n  THE BANK'S OWN PAGE (the cookie, and XSRF-TOKEN in the header):\n      -> 201\n  transfers made: 1, the one the bank's own page asked for",
         narration="A script adds the header itself. [[slnc 300]] The preflight answer allows no other origin, so it is never sent. [[slnc 400]] The bank's own page: two oh one. [[slnc 300]] One transfer made."),
    dict(key='19-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title="The key's thumbprint, and one proof",
         body='thumbprint',
         narration="Fourth demo: tokens bound to a key, with D Pop. [[slnc 400]] On the left, how the token names the key. [[slnc 300]] The app's public key is a point on the P two fifty six curve: two numbers, x and y. [[slnc 300]] It is written as a short J son text, with the members in alphabetical order and no spaces, so everyone gets the same text. [[slnc 600]] That text goes through S H A two fifty six, which gives thirty two bytes. [[slnc 300]] In base sixty four U R L, forty three characters: the thumbprint. [[slnc 300]] Our hand-made value equals the library's. [[slnc 300]] And Keycloak writes the same value into the access token, as c n f dot j k t. [[slnc 700]] On the right, one proof. [[slnc 300]] A small token signed by the key. [[slnc 300]] A random id, used once. [[slnc 300]] The method and the address of this one request. [[slnc 300]] The time. [[slnc 300]] And the hash of the access token. [[slnc 600]] To use the token, you need a fresh proof from the key it names."),
    dict(key='20-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — Tokens bound to a key, with DPoP',
         body='FOUR. Tokens bound to a key, with DPoP.\n  THE APP\'S PUBLIC KEY (P-256; scripts may sign, not read it):\n    {"crv":"P-256","kty":"EC",\n    "x":"bOqwVI5JtA3RETwTVAbTxqNdO2aFvmnlt8HUsq-sRFQ",\n    "y":"gSnq4nM3E1v5GhD4OErnbdU6FvBjTRCsDmfMzaLv3Pc"}\n  THE THUMBPRINT, WORKED OUT (RFC 7638):\n    the lines above: a to z, no spaces, 126 characters\n    SHA-256 of it, 32 bytes, in base64url:\n    lq6Aws5FE-qf5LOaH8K26iRrqGPFaWQ0v8IwPTXG6UU\n    the same as Nimbus\'s computeThumbprint(): true\n  THE TOKEN FROM KEYCLOAK (client bank-spa-dpop):\n    token_type  DPoP\n    cnf.jkt     lq6Aws5FE-qf5LOaH8K26iRrqGPFaWQ0v8IwPTXG6UU\n                = the thumbprint',
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The app's public key, in the text the thumbprint hashes. [[slnc 400]] The thumbprint, worked out, and the same as the library's. [[slnc 400]] And Keycloak's token carries exactly that value."),
    dict(key='21-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — Tokens bound to a key, with DPoP',
         body="FOUR. (continued)\n  THE PROOF FOR ONE REQUEST (a small JWT signed by the key):\n    typ dpop+jwt, alg ES256, jwk <the public key above>\n    jti  <a random UUID>  (used once)\n    htm  GET\n    htu  http://127.0.0.1:<port>/accounts\n    iat  <now>\n    ath  <SHA-256 of the access token, in base64url>\n  THE APP'S OWN REQUEST (Authorization: DPoP <token>, DPoP: <proof>):\n    GET /accounts -> 200 balance 125000 cents",
         narration='The proof for one request, field by field. [[slnc 400]] The port, the time, the id and the hash change on every run, so they are shown as placeholders. [[slnc 400]] With the token and the proof, the app reads the balance.'),
    dict(key='22-act-4-3', kind='console', title='Act Four — Tokens bound to a key, with DPoP',
         body="FOUR. (continued)\n  THE ATTACKS (a script took the token from localStorage):\n    the token alone, as a plain bearer token\n      -> 401 (invalid_token)\n    with a proof from the thief's own key\n      -> 401 (invalid_dpop_proof)\n    the app's own request, copied and sent again\n      -> 401 (invalid_dpop_proof)\n    the app's proof for GET /accounts, used for a transfer\n      -> 401 (invalid_dpop_proof)\n    a proof made 120 s ago (the token is still valid)\n      -> 401 (invalid_dpop_proof)\n    a proof with no ath (it does not name the token)\n      -> 401 (invalid_dpop_proof)",
         narration="The attacks, each refused by Spring's resource server. [[slnc 300]] The token alone. [[slnc 300]] A proof from the thief's key. [[slnc 300]] A copy of the app's request. [[slnc 300]] A proof for another request. [[slnc 300]] An old proof. [[slnc 300]] And a proof that does not name the token."),
    dict(key='23-act-4-4', kind='console', title='Act Four — Tokens bound to a key, with DPoP',
         body="FOUR. (continued)\n  THE REFRESH TOKEN AT KEYCLOAK, BOUND TO THE SAME KEY:\n    with the thief's key       -> refused\n      (DPoP confirmation doesn't match DPoP proof)\n    with no key at all         -> refused\n    the app, with its own key  -> new tokens\n  a stolen token is useless without the key,\n  and the key never leaves the browser",
         narration="At Keycloak, the refresh token is bound to the same key. [[slnc 300]] The thief's key: refused. [[slnc 300]] No key: refused. [[slnc 300]] The app's key: new tokens."),
    dict(key='24-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='What the defences cost',
         body='the_bill',
         narration="Fifth demo: the bill. [[slnc 400]] Four costs, each with the demo's own answer. [[slnc 600]] Top left: two backend servers, each with its own session store. [[slnc 300]] Ana signs in on the first. [[slnc 300]] The second does not know her cookie. [[slnc 300]] So the sessions need a shared store. [[slnc 600]] Top right: one more server, on every call. [[slnc 300]] Someone has to run it, scale it, and keep it up to date. [[slnc 600]] Bottom left: the browser's storage is cleared, and the D Pop key is gone. [[slnc 300]] The token names the old key's thumbprint, and the new key has a different one. [[slnc 300]] So Ana signs in again. [[slnc 600]] Bottom right: a script on the open page asks the key for a proof. [[slnc 300]] It works. [[slnc 300]] D Pop stops theft, not use while the page is open."),
    dict(key='25-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill',
         body='FIVE. The bill.\n  TWO BFF SERVERS, EACH WITH ITS OWN SESSION STORE:\n    Ana signs in on the first; her cookie reaches the second\n      -> 401 (not signed in)\n    so the sessions need a shared store\n  ONE MORE SERVER:\n    every API call passes through the BFF, which must be run,\n    scaled and kept up to date',
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The second server answers Ana's cookie with four oh one, so the sessions need a shared store. [[slnc 400]] And every call passes through one more server."),
    dict(key='26-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill',
         body="FIVE. (continued)\n  THE BROWSER'S STORAGE CLEARED (the DPoP key is lost):\n    the token is bound to    lq6Aws5FE-qf...\n    the new key's thumbprint Vr-SIKG4_2B7...\n      -> 401 (invalid_dpop_proof)\n    so Ana signs in again\n  A SCRIPT ON THE OPEN PAGE ASKS THE KEY FOR A PROOF:\n      -> 200 balance 125000 cents\n      <- DPOP STOPS THEFT, NOT USE WHILE THE PAGE IS OPEN\n  keep scripts off the page (a strict Content-Security-Policy),\n  and keep tokens out of it (the BFF)",
         narration='The storage cleared: the two thumbprints differ, so the old token is refused, and Ana signs in again. [[slnc 400]] A script on the open page still reads the balance. [[slnc 400]] So keep scripts off the page, and keep tokens out of it.'),
    dict(key='27-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['The BFF is another server to run', 'Two BFFs need a shared session store', 'Cookies bring request forgery back', 'DPoP needs support at Keycloak and every API', 'Neither stops a script on the open page'],
         narration='Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] The B F F is another server to run, and one more hop on every call. [[slnc 500]] Two B F F servers need a shared session store. [[slnc 500]] Cookies bring cross-site request forgery back, so the B F F needs its defences. [[slnc 500]] D Po P needs support at the sign-in server, and at every A P I. [[slnc 300]] And neither design stops a script that runs on the open page.'),
    dict(key='28-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=["Tokens in localStorage in the browser's tools", 'A cookie named __Host- something', 'An XSRF-TOKEN cookie and an X-XSRF-TOKEN header', 'Authorization: DPoP and a DPoP header'],
         narration="You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] A token in local storage, seen in the browser's developer tools, is the first demo. [[slnc 500]] A cookie whose name starts with host and two underscores is locked to one site, with no domain. [[slnc 500]] An X S R F token cookie, sent back in a header, is Spring's defence against forged requests. [[slnc 500]] And an authorization header that says D Po P, with a D Po P proof beside it, is a token bound to a key."),
    dict(key='29-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=["A BFF for a bank's web app", 'A __Host- cookie: Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite', 'An XSRF header for every change', 'Refresh on the server', 'DPoP where a token must reach the browser', 'A strict Content-Security-Policy'],
         narration="So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Use a backend for the frontend for a bank's web app. [[slnc 500]] Give the browser one host cookie: secure, http only, and same site strict. [[slnc 300]] Ask for the X S R F header on every request that changes something. [[slnc 500]] Refresh tokens on the server. [[slnc 500]] Where a token must reach the browser, bind it to a key with D Po P. [[slnc 300]] And keep untrusted scripts off the page, with a strict content security policy."),
    dict(key='30-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=["Running: starts the BFF, with the session cookie's rules", 'BffSecurity: the BFF signs Ana in, and checks the XSRF header', 'BffController: passes each call on, with the token added on the server', 'ApiSecurity: the accounts API: scopes, and DPoP switched on', "DpopKey: the browser's key: its thumbprint, and the proofs it signs"],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Keycloak runs in Docker; the accounts A P I and the backend for the frontend are real Spring Boot apps. [[slnc 300]] Here are the classes that matter.'),
    dict(key='31-class-1', kind='code', title='Running — the __Host-bff cookie',
         body='// Running: starts the BFF, with the session cookie\'s rules\n"--server.servlet.session.cookie.name=__Host-bff",\n"--server.servlet.session.cookie.path=/",\n"--server.servlet.session.cookie.secure=true",\n"--server.servlet.session.cookie.http-only=true",\n"--server.servlet.session.cookie.same-site=strict");',
         narration="The session cookie's rules are five Spring Boot settings. [[slnc 300]] The host prefix, the root path, secure, H T T P only, and same site strict."),
    dict(key='32-class-2', kind='code', title='BffSecurity — oauth2Login, and a CSRF token in a cookie',
         body='// BffSecurity: the BFF signs Ana in, and checks the XSRF header\n.oauth2Login(o -> o.authorizationEndpoint(\n        a -> a.authorizationRequestResolver(resolver)))\n.csrf(c -> c.csrfTokenRepository(CookieCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse())\n        .csrfTokenRequestHandler(new CsrfTokenRequestAttributeHandler()))',
         narration='O Auth login signs Ana in on the server, with P K C E. [[slnc 300]] The C S R F token goes into a cookie the page may read. [[slnc 300]] A change must send it back in a header, which another site cannot do.'),
    dict(key='33-class-3', kind='code', title='BffController — the token never leaves the server',
         body='// BffController: passes each call on, with the token added on the server\nString accessToken = client.getAccessToken().getTokenValue();\n...\n        .headers(h -> h.setBearerAuth(accessToken));',
         narration='The authorized client manager gives the access token, refreshed when it has run out. [[slnc 300]] The server adds it to the call to the A P I. [[slnc 300]] The browser never sees it.'),
    dict(key='34-class-4', kind='code', title='ApiSecurity — one line for DPoP',
         body='// ApiSecurity: the accounts API: scopes, and DPoP switched on\n.requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/accounts").hasAuthority("SCOPE_accounts.read")\n.requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/transfers").hasAuthority("SCOPE_payments.write")\n...\n.oauth2ResourceServer(o -> o\n        .jwt(Customizer.withDefaults())\n        .dPoP(Customizer.withDefaults()));',
         narration='Each address needs its scope. [[slnc 300]] And D Pop is one line in Spring Security seven. [[slnc 300]] A bound token then needs a fresh proof from its key.'),
    dict(key='35-class-5', kind='code', title='DpopKey — the thumbprint, by hand',
         body='// DpopKey: the browser\'s key: its thumbprint, and the proofs it signs\nreturn "{\\"crv\\":\\"" + key.getCurve().getName() + "\\",\\"kty\\":\\"EC\\""\n        + ",\\"x\\":\\"" + key.getX() + "\\",\\"y\\":\\"" + key.getY() + "\\"}";\n...\nbyte[] hash = sha256(canonical().getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));\nreturn Base64.getUrlEncoder().withoutPadding().encodeToString(hash);',
         narration="The thumbprint is the S H A two fifty six of a fixed text: four members, in alphabetical order, with no spaces. [[slnc 300]] A test shows it equals the library's own."),
    dict(key='36-class-6', kind='code', title='DpopKey — one proof for one request',
         body='// DpopKey: the browser\'s key: its thumbprint, and the proofs it signs\nJWTClaimsSet.Builder claims = new JWTClaimsSet.Builder().jwtID(jti)\n        .claim("htm", method).claim("htu", url).issueTime(Date.from(issuedAt));\nif (accessToken != null) {\n    byte[] hash = sha256(accessToken.getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII));\n    claims.claim("ath", Base64.getUrlEncoder().withoutPadding().encodeToString(hash));\n}',
         narration='Each proof carries a random id, the method, the address and the time. [[slnc 300]] And the hash of the access token, so it works only with that token.'),
    dict(key='37-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', "are in the repository. Move the BFF's tokens to a", 'shared store and run two BFF servers.'],
         narration="That's tokens in the browser, with a backend for the frontend and D Po P. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] A page that holds tokens gives them to every script on it, so keep the tokens on a server, use cookies with the right attributes, and bind any token that must reach the browser to a key. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Move the B F F's tokens from memory to a shared store, and run two B F F servers behind one address. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

